WHEREAS on July 1, 2026, the Permanent Council of La Francophonie expressed its support for Canada’s bid to host the 21st Francophonie Summit in 2028; and
WHEREAS the Prime Minister has confirmed that the 2028 Francophonie Summit will take place in the National Capital Region, including Ottawa; and
WHEREAS the 2028 Francophonie Summit will bring together representatives of 90 member states and governments of the Organisation internationale de la Francophonie, and represents a historic opportunity to showcase Ottawa as the bilingual capital of Canada and the home of one of North America’s largest French-speaking communities outside Quebec;
WHEREAS Ottawa City Council had communicated its support for the efforts of the bid committee and the Government of Canada to host the Summit through a motion (Motion No. 2026-73-26) approved on January 28, 2026; and
WHEREAS Motion No. 2026-73-26 noted the essential contribution of Ottawa’s Francophone and Francophile community to the city’s cultural, economic, social and institutional development, including that:
WHEREAS the City continues to engage with the Francophone and Francophile community and related organizations, joining the International Association of Francophone Mayors in February 2026; and
WHEREAS by hosting the 2028 Francophonie Summit, Ottawa will serve as a focal point for celebrating the richness and resilience of the French language in Canada and throughout the world; and
WHEREAS the Summit is an opportunity to demonstrate the vitality of Ontario’s Francophone community, highlight Ottawa’s Francophone and Francophile community, and leave a lasting legacy for the community through the strengthening of French-language services and the promotion of the French language within municipal facilities and public spaces;
THEREFORE, BE IT RESOLVED THAT Ottawa City Council proclaim 2028 as the “Year of La Francophonie in Ottawa” in recognition of hosting the 21st Francophonie Summit, as described in this motion;
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED THAT Council direct City staff to collaborate with partner governments, Francophone organizations, cultural and economic institutions, educational establishments, and Ottawa’s Francophone and Francophile communities to develop a municipal program of celebration and outreach for 2028;
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED THAT the City of Ottawa work in collaboration with Ottawa Tourism and the Regroupement des gens d’affaires de la capitale nationale (RGA) to promote Ottawa in French-speaking tourism markets, in association with economic development partners, in order to increase the accessibility and visibility of French-language services throughout the city;
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED THAT the City of Ottawa encourage all community, cultural, educational and economic partners to participate in celebrating the Year of La Francophonie in Ottawa in 2028; and
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED THAT Mayor Sutcliffe provide a copy of this motion to the governments of Canada and Ontario, as well as to the Organisation internationale de la Francophonie.

That the rules of procedure be suspended to consider the following motion to allow staff to engage the relevant expertise to undertake the requested analysis without delay.
WHEREAS over the past several years, Nepean has suffered several extreme weather events including the 2018 tornados; 2022 derecho; the 2023 ice storm; the July 1, 2026 rainstorm and floods; and the August 12, 2026 windstorm; and
WHEREAS the City of Ottawa’s Climate Projections for the National Capital Region (2020) indicate that Ottawa is projected to see rising temperatures, more rainfall and more extreme weather events; and
WHEREAS recent and historical weather patterns suggest that the affected areas of Nepean may experience localized weather and climatic conditions that differ from other parts of the city;
WHEREAS the City of Ottawa uses climate and weather reports when designing its infrastructure standards, infrastructure maintenance routines and resident support programs; and
WHEREAS Infrastructure and Water Services is currently conducting an analysis of the 2026 Rainfall Events that will analyze rainfall, flooding impacts, resident reports, infrastructure performance, watershed conditions, operational information, and previous studies to determine what occurred, why flooding occurred, how municipal systems performed, and what actions may be required to improve flood resilience and inform future planning and infrastructure decisions; and
WHEREAS a meteorologist can provide expert analysis of the physical weather processes that may contribute to observed patterns in thunderstorms and heavy rainfall events;
THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED THAT the City of Ottawa engage a qualified professional with expertise in mesoscale meteorology and urban climatology to analyze recent and historical weather patterns and events in Nepean and across Ottawa, using the best available Ottawa-specific and localized data;
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED THAT the specialist be funded through existing rate funded programs; and
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED THAT the specialist’s findings be made public and used to inform the overall analysis and recommendations arising from the review of the 2026 rainfall events.

WHEREAS members of the Canadian Armed Forces have served Canada with distinction in conflicts and military operations around the world, often facing hostile and life-threatening conditions, significant personal risk, and the possibility of physical and psychological injury;
WHEREAS the nature of warfare and Canadian military operations has evolved considerably since the Korean War, and the formal terminology used to describe a military operation does not necessarily reflect the conditions experienced by the Canadian Armed Forces members who served in it;WHEREAS more than 4,000 Canadian Armed Forces members served in the Persian Gulf region during the 1990–1991 Gulf War as part of the international coalition formed in response to Iraq’s invasion of Kuwait, including Canadian personnel who served in active combat roles;
WHEREAS Gulf War veterans and their advocates have long sought recognition of their service as wartime service, rather than its classification as Special Duty service, reflecting the risks, operational conditions and sacrifices experienced by those who served;
WHEREAS veterans of the Gulf War, Afghanistan and other modern Canadian military operations deserve recognition that reflects the actual nature and conditions of their service and the sacrifices they and their families made on behalf of Canada;
WHEREAS Bill S-246, the Wartime Service Recognition Act, would require the Government of Canada to establish a national framework and objective criteria for recognizing military service as wartime service, including consideration of the level of risk, exposure to hostile or life-threatening conditions, the nature and intensity of military operations, and exposure to conditions capable of causing physical or psychological injury;
WHEREAS Bill S-246 would require the Minister of National Defence to review all Canadian Armed Forces operations conducted since July 27, 1953, against these objective criteria and recommend the designation of qualifying service as wartime service;
WHEREAS Bill S-246 specifically provides that the formal label or description applied to a military operation at the time it was conducted cannot be used as a criterion in determining whether that service constitutes wartime service;
WHEREAS this recognition would be symbolic and commemorative and would not, in itself, create any new entitlement to financial benefits under federal legislation;
WHEREAS Ottawa is home to thousands of serving Canadian Armed Forces members, veterans and military families, whose service and contributions form an important part of our city’s identity;
THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED THAT Ottawa City Council express its support for Bill S-246, the Wartime Service Recognition Act, and its objective of ensuring that Canadian Armed Forces members and veterans, including veterans of the 1990–1991 Gulf War, Afghanistan and other modern military operations, receive fair, consistent and meaningful recognition based upon the actual conditions in which they served;
BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED THAT the Mayor, on behalf of Ottawa City Council, write to the Prime Minister of Canada, the Minister of National Defence, the Minister of Veterans Affairs, the Speaker of the House of Commons, and Ottawa-area Members of Parliament to communicate Council’s support for Bill S-246 and encourage its timely consideration and passage by Parliament.

WHEREAS the buildings on the parcel of land known as 344 McRae Avenue is owned by the City of Ottawa, and;
WHEREAS the City will be demolishing the buildings as soon as approvals are in place to allow for the start of expansion of Livmore Park in 2026 and be completed in 2027; and
WHEREAS the site is subject to a Demolition Control application and the normal approval process under demolition control could unduly delay the project and restrict meeting the timelines for tendering the project; and
WHEREAS, by waiving demolition control approval, the City will not receive$6200.48 (includes $1274.64 Legal Fee + HST) which is the fee associated with a demolition control application; and
WHEREAS the site would still be subject to a demolition permit;
THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED that Council waive demolition control approval and associated demolition control fees, to allow the City to proceed directly to seeking a demolition permit for the buildings on 344 McRae Avenue.
